      "created_at": "2019-04-09T11:51:04Z",
      "updated_at": "2019-04-09T11:51:04Z",
      "body": "Thanks, but my main point was: Having personal information like names and home locations stored unencrypted and bound to the same directory if storage of the maps on external SD card is used, is a major threat in case of robbery or loss or unauthorized access to the SD card, especially as the user usually does not know that this information stored in plain text on SD card. Thus, the mandatory link between map storage and user data should be removed.\r\n\r\n> I will vote against this issue cause it seriously harms usability between apps which user can control.\r\n\r\nThis I don't understand, neither technical (in terms of Android common practice or app interfaces) nor functional: The user is asked where to store the *maps*, not the *personal data* that is entered into Osmand. \r\n\r\n> I think there is no connection to EU privacy law (GDPR) cause this data is solely stored on user device and has no connection OsmAnd company.\r\n\r\nGDPR and most other privacy laws cover all handling, storage, and protection of personal data, not only internet transmission. If a user saves data within favourites that reveals personal data of other people (e. g. name, geo location, relationship, reason of visiting, medical attributes of these people) within Osmand, and Osmand saves this on an exposed SD card without knowledge or consent of the user, there is indeed a GDPR issue that could be easily avoided, no matter if the Osmand company or the user could be legally held responsible for it. If the Osmand company knows about the issue but does not address it, this would be irresponsible to me.\r\n",
      "author_association": "NONE",
